---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Where does BundleEmbedder get runtime.wasm?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'From your app bundle. BundleEmbedder in traverse-embedder-web loads runtime/runtime.wasm plus runtime/runtime.wasm.sha256 from the bundle, verifies the digest, and fails closed at init if the file is missing. The npm package does not ship runtime.wasm. Since November 4, 2026 you can download it from an artifact-only GitHub release that matches your traverse-embedder-web version: runtime-wasm-v0.16.2 (sha256 b313b5c5c11291d9e9669125a0a9a77aa42a911b15d431a73e86b17c874e298c) or runtime-wasm-v0.16.1 (sha256 7a322a17e3fd58f8bd26e915dd81bad68732500dc981499bd280a6d2e6507d52). Each release carries runtime.wasm, runtime.wasm.sha256 and runtime-release.json. traverse#1784 was closed by traverse#1797, which makes every future product tag publish runtime-wasm-vX.Y.Z before the product release. The file is also committed at runtime/runtime.wasm on each release tag.',
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/questions/what-is-one-shared-runtime-wasm.html', label: 'What is one shared runtime.wasm in Traverse?' },
  { href: '/questions/does-the-rust-embedder-use-the-same-runtime-wasm.html', label: 'Does the Rust embedder use the same runtime.wasm?' },
  { href: '/questions/does-catalog-model-execute-run-on-published-traverse-packages.html', label: 'Does catalog model.execute run on published Traverse packages?' },
  { href: '/questions/how-do-i-upgrade-to-traverse-v0-16-0.html', label: 'How do I upgrade to Traverse v0.16.x?' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Where does BundleEmbedder get runtime.wasm?"
  description="From your app bundle, digest-checked. npm traverse-embedder-web doesn't ship it. Download it from the runtime-wasm-v0.16.2 GitHub release that matches your package, and check the sha256."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/where-does-bundleembedder-get-runtime-wasm.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> from your app bundle. <code>BundleEmbedder</code> loads <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code> and <code>runtime/runtime.wasm.sha256</code> from the bundle you give it, checks the digest, and fails closed at <code>init</code> if the file isn't there. To get the file, download it from the <code>runtime-wasm</code> release that matches your <code>traverse-embedder-web</code> version.</p>

  <h2>What ships where (v0.16.2)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>npm <code>traverse-embedder-web</code></strong> doesn't include <code>runtime.wasm</code>. Its README says so: the orchestrator belongs to the app bundle.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/runtime-wasm-v0.16.2"><code>runtime-wasm-v0.16.2</code></a></strong> and <strong><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/runtime-wasm-v0.16.1"><code>runtime-wasm-v0.16.1</code></a></strong> are artifact-only GitHub releases, published November 4, 2026. Each has <code>runtime.wasm</code>, <code>runtime.wasm.sha256</code> and <code>runtime-release.json</code>. They are never marked Latest.</li>
    <li><strong>The tag still has it.</strong> <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code> and <code>runtime/runtime-release.json</code> are committed at each release tag, with the same bytes.</li>
    <li>Before November 4 the last <code>runtime-wasm-v*</code> release was <code>runtime-wasm-v0.11.0</code>, so v0.16.0 has no matching release. Take v0.16.0 from its tag.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to do</h2>
  <p>Download the three files for your version and check the digest before you put the file in your bundle. For v0.16.2:</p>
  <pre><code>BASE=https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/download/runtime-wasm-v0.16.2
curl -fsSLO $BASE/runtime.wasm
curl -fsSLO $BASE/runtime.wasm.sha256
curl -fsSLO $BASE/runtime-release.json
sha256sum runtime.wasm
# expect b313b5c5c11291d9e9669125a0a9a77aa42a911b15d431a73e86b17c874e298c</code></pre>
  <p><code>runtime-release.json</code> for v0.16.2 reads <code>runtime_version</code> 0.16.2, <code>bridge_version</code> 1.1.0, <code>bridge_abi_version</code> 10100, and the same sha256. <code>runtime.wasm.sha256</code> already holds <code>sha256:&lt;hex&gt;</code>, the form the bundle expects, so it can go straight into <code>runtime/</code>. For v0.16.1 use <code>runtime-wasm-v0.16.1</code> and expect <code>7a322a17e3fd58f8bd26e915dd81bad68732500dc981499bd280a6d2e6507d52</code>. We downloaded the v0.16.2 file on November 5 and the digest matched.</p>
  <p>Or build it yourself from the tagged source with Rust 1.95 or later. Either way you end up with one shared <code>runtime.wasm</code> that every web and Node host loads; native Rust hosts link <code>traverse-runtime</code> directly and don't need the file.</p>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1784">traverse#1784</a> asked how apps should get the file. <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1797">traverse#1797</a> closed it: every product tag now publishes <code>runtime-wasm-vX.Y.Z</code>, and the product GitHub Release waits for it (Spec 048 1.5.0, Decision 123). That wiring is on main; the v0.16.1 and v0.16.2 releases were backfilled from their tags, and the first automatic run will be the next product tag. npm still doesn't ship the file.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
